{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789387400,"nonce":"GxumrsPZJYh0S9Je2niRQtiCiaI=","title":"M:N fibers: run Wren on every core the way Ash's tasks do","message":"Ash's scheduler, now `caribou::sched`, is M:N: a process runs several worlds, one per OS thread, over the one heap. A task is placed on the least-loaded world when it is spawned and never migrates (a krio fiber is `!Send`), worlds exchange wake and spawn commands through per-world endpoints, and a collection stops every world at its safepoints. Haxe threads and Ash's compiled thread bodies run on the pool that way. Wren under caribou runs one VM on one world: every fiber, and every call from Haxe, on the thread that made the VM.\n\nRecorded now so the shape is decided before the fiber stack is reworked (c4d5a40781314bb30d611838e6e66efead4f73a632c71372ebd363444b6294ea, 3378b4e75b85161a836f818d92ad000029d692a23280c955ab2a9683fa9d8181, and the fiber-boundary guard caribou wants for a Haxe throw inside Wren). Too early to build; not too early to keep in view.\n\nWhat stands in the way, in wren_lift:\n\n1. One execution context per VM. `vm.fiber`, the error state, `pending_fiber_action`, the JIT thread state (`jit_state()`, already thread-local) and the interpreter's frame stacks are the running fiber's, but they hang off the one `VM`. Running fibers on two threads means splitting the VM into what is shared (interner, modules, classes and their method tables, module variables, the engine's code and tier state) and what is per running thread (the current fiber, its error, its JIT state), the way the core keeps a world per thread over one heap.\n2. Shared mutable state with no discipline yet: module variables, static fields, class method tables (`bind_host`, tier-up installs), inline caches, and the tier manager's counters are written from wherever a fiber runs. Some are benign to race (a counter, an IC), some are not (a method table growing).\n3. The heap. Under caribou the core heap already serves many threads (per-thread TLABs, stop-the-world at safepoints), and the Wren heap record's pins list is the one thing touched only by the VM's thread. On wren_lift's own collectors, none of this exists.\n4. Wren's own semantics. A `Fiber` is a coroutine: `call`, `transfer` and `yield` hand control over explicitly, and a program written that way has no data races because nothing runs at once. Making fibers parallel silently would break that. Parallelism wants its own surface — a fiber spawned onto the pool, or an isolate — with the ownership rules stated.\n\nTwo shapes, in the order they become possible:\n\n- **Isolates.** One VM per world, tasks pinned, and values crossing between VMs as caribou values already cross between languages (by copy for strings and numbers, by handle for objects). This needs nothing from the list above: it is what the core's multiple worlds already are, plus a Wren-side API to spawn one and talk to it. It gives every core to a program that partitions its work.\n- **M:N fibers on one VM.** The split in (1), the discipline in (2), and a spawn-onto-pool primitive for (4), with `Fiber.call`/`transfer` keeping their meaning for fibers on the same world. This is the Ash shape.\n\nEither way the scheduler is the core's: a Wren fiber is already a stackful task on it, and placing one on another world is `spawn_fiber_on_pool`.","files":null}]}